Challenges and Obstacles
What challenges or obstacles have you encountered during the past six months, and how have you addressed or managed them?

One of the key challenges during the review period has been maintaining reliable data services while the underlying technology, infrastructure and business requirements continue to evolve.

Changes such as network configuration and Spark runtime upgrades can affect established pipelines and ETL notebooks. When these issues have occurred, I have taken a structured troubleshooting approach by reviewing pipeline execution, identifying root causes, assessing dependencies, updating code where required and validating the resulting data before downstream consumption.

Where possible, I have also used these incidents as opportunities to optimise the affected processes rather than simply restoring previous functionality.

Another area that has required careful management has been balancing a broad range of Data Engineering priorities across operational support and strategic project delivery.

As the Data Engineering resource supporting multiple areas of the business, my responsibilities have ranged from maintaining and troubleshooting critical data pipelines and supporting downstream reporting, through to progressing the FTP data model, investigating data-quality issues and responding to evolving requirements from Insight & Analytics, FTP/Business Change and Office Services.

I have managed these competing priorities by focusing on business impact and urgency, proactively monitoring critical data processes and maintaining effective communication with stakeholders. This has enabled me to respond to operational issues while continuing to progress longer-term objectives around data modelling, governance, security and platform improvement.

Another challenge has been diagnosing reporting problems where the visible failure originates from an upstream dependency. For example, while supporting the Office Services Door Control reporting solution, I investigated issues across Power BI and the underlying Synapse data source. Where specialist investigation was required, I proactively engaged Microsoft technical support to support root-cause analysis.

The FTP data model has also presented technical challenges. During validation and structural review, limitations were identified within aspects of the initial design. I have approached these constructively by investigating the underlying modelling issues and taking ownership of elements of the remediation, including creating and modifying data objects and optimising aspects of the model.

Data quality has similarly required continued investigation because issues can originate from source systems, schema definitions, transformation processes or changing business requirements. My approach has been to analyse and document issues, assess their potential downstream impact, communicate findings with relevant stakeholders and propose potential remediation.

I have also supported greater team resilience through knowledge transfer, sharing knowledge around platform monitoring, pipeline troubleshooting, FTP reporting and data-quality processes.

Managing this breadth of responsibilities has further strengthened my ability to prioritise effectively, work independently, take end-to-end ownership and maintain focus across immediate operational requirements and longer-term strategic objectives.

